---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Card, Table } from "webcoreui/astro";

const tableHeadings = [
    "Spell Cast",
    "Cards Seen",
    "Chance to find at least 1 of a 4-of",
    "Chance to find at least 1 of a 3-of",
];
const tableData = [
    ["<strong>Frantic Search</strong>", "2", "15.1%", "11.4%"],
    ["<strong>Attunement</strong> (1 activation)", "3", "22.2%", "16.9%"],
    ["<strong>Fact or Fiction</strong>", "5", "35.3%", "27.3%"],
];
---

<Layout title="Mulligan Guide - Replenish Guide"
    description="Learn when to keep or mulligan your opening hand with the Replenish deck. Explore probability tables, mathematical frameworks, and real-world case studies for MTG Premodern.">
    <PageHeader
        title="Mulligan Guide"
        description="Knowing when to keep and when to throw back your opening hand is one of the most difficult and rewarding skills with Replenish. Let's break down the math and the theory."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/attunement.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Attunement"
        imageCaption="Attunement by Randy Gallegos"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Advanced Mulligan Theory (Post-Tide)" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            <strong>Reduced Topdeck Reliance:</strong> Following the banning of <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a>, the deck transitioned from possessing eight autonomous hand-fixers to only four (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a>). Because your statistical probability of drawing out of an uncoordinated hand has literally halved, <strong>you are mathematically obligated to mulligan far more aggressively in Game 1 in the dark.</strong> You can no longer afford to retain slow, speculative starting hands and rely on perfect topdecks to bail you out.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>The Early-Turn Rule:</strong> When evaluating opening hands in the blind, a competitive pilot is willing to rely on topdecking exactly <em>one</em> specific operational element during early draw steps (such as hitting a third land drop or drawing an early interaction spell)—never more than one.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
            <strong>Minimum Keep Thresholds:</strong> A viable championship hand must guarantee functional mana delivery paired directly with either strong early interaction, an active selection engine, or a reliable tutor.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-success);">Keepable Architectures (Snap Keeps)</h3>
        <p>Assuming functional colored mana production, standard keepable formations in the blind include:</p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Active Engine:</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a> + lands. The absolute gold-standard opening, immediately filtering redundant draws and insulating resources against discard disruption.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Natural Combo In Hand:</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a> + <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a>.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Tutor + Key Piece:</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a> + any foundational component (Opal, Wave, or Replenish). Intuition reliably locates whatever missing link remains, fully justifying its turn-three investment.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Dual Interaction + Setup:</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a> + at least 2 interaction pieces (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares">Swords to Plowshares</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a>). Buys critical operational time to locate selection engines naturally.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Interaction + Discard Engine:</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> + <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a> + an expensive permanent to dump into the graveyard (Opal, Wave, or Decree).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Early Board Control:</strong> <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a> + <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div style="background-color: var(--w-color-primary-95); border: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-80); border-radius: var(--w-lg-radius); padding: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
            <h4 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">The Ancient Tomb Acceleration Factor</h4>
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a> dramatically accelerates game-plan pacing, transforming otherwise sluggish combinations into automatic, tier-1 operational keeps. Consider the following <strong>automatic keeps</strong> whenever Ancient Tomb anchors your starting mana:
            </p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;"><code>Swords to Plowshares</code> + <code>Intuition</code> + <code>Ancient Tomb</code></li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.35rem;"><code>Frantic Search</code> + <code>Intuition</code> + <code>Ancient Tomb</code></li>
                <li><code>Mana Leak</code> + <code>Intuition</code> + <code>Ancient Tomb</code></li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-danger);">Blind Mulligan Traps (Unviable Starts)</h3>
        <p>A competitive pilot aggressively folds the following structured traps when evaluating in the dark:</p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>The Passive Hand (No Interaction or Engine):</strong> 4 Lands + <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a> + <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a> + <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a>. Requires too many speculative, consecutive topdeck alignments to execute anything proactive before falling fatally behind.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>The Partial Resource Trap:</strong> 5 Lands + 2 spot interaction spells. Presents an exceptionally high statistical probability of drawing uncastable, redundant combo weights (Opal, Replenish, Decree) without any mechanism to discard them or accelerate their development, losing inevitable attrition games.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Orphaned Combo Pieces:</strong> Holding <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> without an active enabling engine (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a>, or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a>). Replenish is strictly dead weight during early sequences when uncoupled from graveyard preparation.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div
            style="margin-top: 3rem; background-color: var(--w-color-primary-95); border: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-80); border-radius: var(--w-lg-radius); padding: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2rem);"
        >
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">
                Case Study: The Great Mulligan Debate
            </h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem;">
                <em
                    >This is a real community debate over a specific
                    hypothetical opening hand that highlights how top players
                    evaluate risk vs. reward in the post-ban format.</em
                >
            </p>

            <p>
                <strong>The Scenario:</strong> Game 1, on the play, in the dark.<br
                />
                <strong>The Hand:</strong> 4 Lands (including Ancient Tomb), <code
                    >Seal of Cleansing</code
                >, <code>Decree of Silence</code>, <code>Frantic Search</code>.
            </p>

            <p>
                <em
                    >Do you keep or mulligan? The community was split right down
                    the middle.</em
                >
            </p>

            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-success);">
                Argument 1: The "Keep" Camp
            </h4>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Hitting Land Drops:</strong> Historically, Replenish loses
                    games when it stumbles on mana. This hand guarantees you hit your
                    first 4 land drops.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>The Frantic Math:</strong> You have 15 "outs" left in
                    the deck that turn this into a winning hand (Replenish, Wave,
                    Attunement, Intuition). Between your natural draw steps and Frantic
                    Search, you see 4 new cards by turn 3. Statistically, you have
                    roughly a 75% chance to hit at least one of those outs.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Flexibility:</strong> The hand has game against control
                    (Seal + Decree + Lands) and discard decks (redundant lands). Its
                    only glaring weakness is a fast aggro start.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-danger);">
                Argument 2: The "Mulligan" Camp
            </h4>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Frantic Search is not an engine:</strong> Relying on it
                    as your only path to victory is dangerous because it is card disadvantage.
                    If you whiff, you are completely out of gas.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>The 50/50 Coinflip:</strong> Looking at just the immediate
                    Frantic Search draw, you have roughly a 50% chance to brick completely.
                    A fresh hand of 7 cards has better overall odds of providing a
                    coherent gameplan.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Seal is not a turn-buyer:</strong> While Seal is a fantastic
                    maindeck card, unlike Swords to Plowshares or Mana Leak, it does
                    not buy you time against early creature pressure. Keeping a hand
                    with Seal as your only interaction is very risky in the dark.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h4 style="margin-top: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
                The Verdict
            </h4>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                There is no absolute "right" answer, but this debate reveals a
                core tension: Risk-averse players prefer to guarantee their land
                drops and trust the math of their cantrips, while aggressive
                mulliganers believe that with the weaker topdecks post-ban, you
                can no longer afford to keep hands that don't proactively
                dictate the pace of the game.
            </p>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Math Behind Replenish" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            We can calculate the probability of finding a specific missing combo
            piece (e.g., Replenish) when casting our card selection spells using
            a hypergeometric calculator.
        </p>
        <p>
            Assuming we are on the play and we cast our spell on Turn 3 (so we
            have drawn 9 cards total: 7 starting + 2 draw steps). There are 51
            cards left in the deck.
        </p>

        <div
            style="margin-bottom: 2rem; margin-top: 1rem; overflow-x: auto; width: 100%; max-width: 100%;"
        >
            <Table
                headings={tableHeadings}
                data={tableData}
                hover={true}
                striped="row"
            />
        </div>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 4rem;">
            This is why keeping hands without any engine or cantrip is
            dangerous. A single Frantic Search only gives you a ~15% chance to
            find that one specific card you need to survive. The more you know
            these percentages, the better your in-game sequencing will be.
        </p>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
